🔹 1. DataFrame

Definition:
A DataFrame is a distributed collection of data organized into named columns, similar to a table in a database.

from pyspark.sql import SparkSession

spark = SparkSession.builder.appName("Practice").getOrCreate()

data = [(1, "Alice", 25, 50000)]
columns = ["id", "name", "age", "salary"]

df = spark.createDataFrame(data, columns)
df.show()
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
🔹 2. select()

Definition:
Used to select specific columns from a DataFrame.

df.select("name", "salary").show()
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
🔹 3. filter()

Definition:
Used to filter rows based on a condition.

from pyspark.sql.functions import col

df.filter(col("salary") > 50000).show()
----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
🔹 4. withColumn()

Definition:
Used to add a new column or modify an existing column.

df = df.withColumn("bonus", col("salary") * 0.1)
----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
🔹 5. withColumnRenamed()

Definition:
Used to rename a column.

df = df.withColumnRenamed("name", "employee_name")
-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
🔹 6. drop()

Definition:
Used to remove a column from a DataFrame.

df = df.drop("age")
-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
🔹 7. Immutability

Definition:
PySpark DataFrames are immutable, meaning they cannot be changed after creation. Every operation returns a new DataFrame.
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
🔹 8. Transformation

Definition:
A transformation is an operation that returns a new DataFrame and does not execute immediately.

df_filtered = df.filter(col("salary") > 50000)
df_selected = df.select("name", "salary")
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
🔹 9. Action

Definition:
An action triggers execution and returns a result.

df.show()
df.count()
df.collect()
-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
🔹 10. Lazy Evaluation

Definition:
Lazy evaluation means Spark delays execution of transformations until an action is called.

Example
df1 = df.filter(col("salary") > 50000)
df2 = df1.select("name")

df2.show()
------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
🎯 Key Takeaways
DataFrames are immutable
Transformations are lazy
Actions trigger execution
Spark optimizes execution using lazy evaluation
